# THE FLOORED SIGN LAW, FOLDED — the same four cells as `examples/table/divsign.id`
# reached through the other implementation, plus the `/` row that is still open.
#
# Every operand here is a LITERAL, so `src/comptime.zig`'s `evalNumeric` answers
# and the backend never sees a division. The sibling's operands are parameters
# and the backend answers there. The four leading columns are common to both
# files ON PURPOSE: fold-and-realize agreement is then a measured fact, and it
# is worth measuring because `evalNumeric` held BOTH laws at once — its
# `native_fold` branch answered `@divTrunc`/`@rem` and its ordinary branch
# `@divFloor`/`@mod`, twelve lines apart, so which law a program got depended on
# whether a fold happened to be running under the native switch. `gate/divisor.sh`
# §4 asks the same question from the compiler side and is the stronger form of
# it; it exits 2 before reaching §4 on a host with no direct-native realization,
# which is every host these two fixtures currently run on.
#
# THE CITATION IS RETRACTED. This header settled `%` from `docs/rulings.md`
# § "Modulo and floor division", and that document has never been added on any
# branch — `git log --all --diff-filter=A -- docs/rulings.md` is empty. Before
# that it cited `§12 B-4`, which exists in no document either. The law's only
# home in this tree is now the measurement; `examples/table/divsign.id` carries
# the full statement and the conviction table.
#
# THE LAST TWO COLUMNS ARE THE OPEN ROW. `/` on two integers truncates toward
# zero HERE, and `-3 -3` against the `-4 -4` two columns left of it is the
# entire distance between the two laws — one line, same operands. It is not
# settled: Lua's `/` is float division, `sema` types `i64 / i64` as `f64`
# (`GAP-065`), and a relation declared `: i64` cannot spell it at all, which is
# why the sibling cannot carry this row. Half a numeric law is worse than none,
# so it is stated as open rather than settled by momentum.
#
# A zero divisor spelled as a literal is refused here by `sema` before any of
# this — "division by zero: the divisor of '//' is the literal 0" — so the
# runtime guard law §62 requires is measured through the sibling's parameters,
# not here.
#
# Measured by `gate/divsign.sh`.
# expect: -4 -4 1 -1 -3 -3
# @corpus current
a = -7 // 2
b = 7 // -2
c = -7 % 2
d = 7 % -2
e = -7 / 2
f = 7 / -2
print("{a} {b} {c} {d} {e} {f}")
0
